# Journal

########## Week 1 – Verilog Basics ##########

### Goal

Learn the fundamentals of Verilog and become comfortable writing simple combinational logic modules.

### Completed Projects

-  AND Gate
-  OR Gate
-  XOR Gate
-  2:1 Multiplexer
-  1:2 Demultiplexer
-  2-to-4 Decoder

### What I Learned

During this week I learned the basic structure of a Verilog module and how hardware description languages differ from traditional programming languages.

I became familiar with:

- `module`
- `input` and `output`
- `wire`
- `assign`
- combinational logic
- truth tables
- writing testbenches
- verifying designs using simulation

I also learned how common digital building blocks such as multiplexers, demultiplexers and decoders are used to route and select data in digital systems.

### Challenges

At the beginning it took some time to adjust to thinking in terms of hardware rather than sequential software execution.

Another challenge was understanding the purpose of the different logic components instead of only memorizing their truth tables.

### How I Solved Them

I used waveform simulations together with truth tables to verify every design.

Reading the documentation and comparing the behavior of each circuit helped me understand why each component exists and where it is used.

### Reflection

This week gave me a solid introduction to digital design.

I now feel comfortable writing simple combinational circuits in Verilog and verifying them using simulations.

Although the individual projects were relatively small, they introduced many of the concepts that will be reused throughout the rest of this learning journey.

### Next Step

Sequential logic.

I will learn how clocks, flip-flops, registers and finite state machines work before moving on to larger computer components.

